Chapter: E1000Phy
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 MDIC, the management door to the PHY, and the first test in this tree to
 reach a PHY register at all. Until the model grew one, every PHY-shaped
 defect in this driver was invisible here by construction: the model
 granted link on CTRL.SLU alone, so a driver that never touched the PHY
 passed, and on the I219 -- a PCH-integrated MAC whose PHY is reached
 only through MDIC -- that is exactly the failure that would have been
 silent.

 Four readings, and each is chosen so its two candidate answers look
 different rather than so it can pass.

 The PHY id is read back through MDIC. A model with no PHY reads zero on
 an unclaimed register, and zero is also what a bus with nothing on it
 reads, so the id is asserted as a specific non-zero value: it separates
 "reached the PHY" from "read a hole". It is the MODEL's id and is not
 measured off any real part, so this asserts the transaction worked, not
 that we recognise an I219.

 The address arm is the one that proves the transaction is addressed at
 all. A read of a PHY address nobody answers must come back as the error
 sentinel and not as zero, because a driver that folds those together
 cannot tell an absent PHY from a register holding zero. This is the
 arm that would fail if e1000-mdic-frame ignored its address field.

 Auto-negotiation is asserted as a TRANSITION, not a level. BMSR's
 auto-negotiation-complete bit is clear before the driver restarts
 negotiation and set afterwards, so a model that simply always reported
 done would fail the first half. A single reading after bring-up would
 have been satisfied by a constant.

 The link arm runs under `-e1000-phy-link`, which makes STATUS.LU require
 the PHY to have finished negotiating rather than merely CTRL.SLU. That
 flag is off by default so every existing green keeps the floor it was
 measured against; here it is on, and it is what makes this a test of the
 PHY path rather than of SLU.

Section: Fixtures

  found-dev : PciScanResult -> Maybe PciDevice
  found-dev (scan) = e1000-find scan

Section: Judges

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

 Read straight off the bar, before any bring-up, so the pre-negotiation
 reading is genuinely pre-negotiation.

  bar-of : PciDevice -> Integer
  bar-of (dev) =
   let enabled = pci-enable-device dev
   in (pci-parse-bar (dev.pci-bus) (dev.pci-dev) (dev.pci-func) 0).bar-base

  phy-id-nonzero : PciDevice -> Boolean
  phy-id-nonzero (dev) = e1000-phy-read (bar-of dev) e1000-phy-id1 > 0

 Address 2 is not e1000-phy-addr and nothing answers there. The reading
 must be the error sentinel and not zero.

  bad-addr-is-error : PciDevice -> Boolean
  bad-addr-is-error (dev) = e1000-phy-read-at (bar-of dev) 2 e1000-phy-id1 < 0

  aneg-before : PciDevice -> Boolean
  aneg-before (dev) =
   bit-and (e1000-phy-read (bar-of dev) e1000-phy-bmsr) e1000-bmsr-aneg-done /= 0

  aneg-after : PciDevice -> Boolean
  aneg-after (dev) =
   let mmio = bar-of dev
   in let b = e1000-phy-bring-up mmio
   in bit-and (e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-bmsr) e1000-bmsr-aneg-done /= 0

  link-after-init : PciDevice -> Boolean
  link-after-init (dev) = e1000-has-link (e1000-init dev)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let scan = pci-scan-bus 0
    in act
      when found-dev scan
        is Just (dev) -> act
          print-line-uni ("phy id nonzero  : " & yn (phy-id-nonzero dev))
          print-line-uni ("bad addr errors : " & yn (bad-addr-is-error dev))
          print-line-uni ("aneg before     : " & yn (aneg-before dev))
          print-line-uni ("aneg after      : " & yn (aneg-after dev))
          print-line-uni ("link under gate : " & yn (link-after-init dev))
        end
        is None -> print-line-uni ("candidate found : no")
    end
  end

